esp32 UART: keep what the chardev does not take, rather than drop it

The UART model (also the S3's and C3's, which inherit it) wrote each byte
to its chardev as the firmware pushed it into the TX FIFO, ignored whether
the write worked, and dropped the byte either way. On a Unix socket
chardev every write is charged against the socket's buffer as a whole
packet, so only a few hundred one-byte writes fit; whenever the reader fell
behind (a busy host, or a reader that was not reading the console just
then) the rest of the output was silently lost. esp32bench's pin tests saw
lines lose their start ("-gpio in 4=1") and boot logs cut mid-word.

What the backend does not take now stays in the FIFO and goes when the
backend has room, through a chardev watch, as QEMU's other UART models do;
the firmware waits for room in the FIFO, as on a slow line. Bytes still go
out as they are pushed, so timing is as before. With no backend connected,
or one that cannot be watched, they are still dropped, so the firmware
never waits for a reader that is not there. The picsimlab TX callback
still sees every byte.

esp32bench reads the console socket all the time (esp32bench_sim::uart),
so a firmware does not wait on it. Sending the FIFO in batches, from a
timer or from the main loop, was tried and dropped: it slowed the S3 with
-icount about three times, and a timer version sometimes stopped output
for good on a heavily loaded host.

